# Unreal Home Wizard

Turn photographs of a room, apartment, or house into a reviewable, walkable
Unreal Engine project with Codex.

This is a guided production workflow for complete beginners. It does not promise
a measured digital twin from photographs alone, and it does not silently install
Unreal Engine or accept third-party terms for the user.

## Install in Codex

Paste this into a new Codex task:

```text
Install the Unreal Home Wizard skill from:
https://github.com/amirmushichge/unreal-home-wizard/tree/v0.2.0/skills/unreal-home-wizard
```

After Codex confirms installation, restart Codex and paste:

```text
$unreal-home-wizard - start a new project from scratch.
```

The wizard asks one simple question at a time. If photographs already exist, it
asks the user to attach them. Technical setup is inspected before construction.

## What is automatic

- installing the Codex skill from this public repository;
- checking the local Windows or macOS system, Codex, Unreal, disk, memory, GPU,
  and project setup;
- creating and running reviewed Unreal Editor Python scripts after authorization;
- organizing private references, evidence, matched cameras, defects, and results;
- repeated photo-match and physical-logic QA.

## What still needs the user

- signing in to Epic Games and accepting Epic's terms;
- approving the multi-gigabyte Unreal Engine installation;
- approving purchases or third-party asset downloads;
- supplying photographs and confirming publication rights;
- approving the rough layout and final result.

## Requirements

- Windows 10 22H2 (64-bit) or newer; Windows 11 is recommended by Epic; or
- an Apple Silicon Mac running macOS Sonoma 14 or newer for the macOS beta path;
- Codex desktop or Codex CLI with local workspace access;
- Epic Games Launcher and Unreal Engine 5.8 for the tested path;
- recommended for Windows UE5 authoring: 32 GB RAM, 8 GB or more graphics
  memory, a DirectX 12-compatible GPU, and current drivers;
- recommended for macOS: App
